public with sharing class ContactLookupAction {

    @InvocableMethod(label='Find Contact by Email')
    public static List<Result> find(List<Request> requests) {
        List<Result> results = new List<Result>();

        // Step 1: Normalise each request's email (trim, detect blank/null)
        Map<Integer, String> indexToEmail = new Map<Integer, String>();
        Set<String> emailSet = new Set<String>();

        for (Integer i = 0; i < requests.size(); i++) {
            String email = requests[i].email;
            if (email != null) {
                email = email.trim();
            }
            if (String.isNotBlank(email)) {
                indexToEmail.put(i, email);
                emailSet.add(email);
            }
        }

        // Step 2: Single SOQL query to fetch all matching contacts
        // Ordered by CreatedDate so the first row per email is the earliest.
        Map<String, Contact> bestByLowerEmail = new Map<String, Contact>();
        if (!emailSet.isEmpty()) {
            List<Contact> contacts = [
                SELECT Id, Email, CreatedDate
                FROM Contact
                WHERE Email IN :emailSet
                ORDER BY CreatedDate ASC
            ];
            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                if (c.Email != null) {
                    String lowerKey = c.Email.toLowerCase();
                    // Because of ORDER BY CreatedDate ASC, the first contact
                    // we encounter for a given (lowercased) email is the earliest.
                    if (!bestByLowerEmail.containsKey(lowerKey)) {
                        bestByLowerEmail.put(lowerKey, c);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        // Step 3: Build one Result per Request, preserving input order
        for (Integer i = 0; i < requests.size(); i++) {
            Result r = new Result();
            String email = indexToEmail.get(i);
            if (email != null) {
                Contact c = bestByLowerEmail.get(email.toLowerCase());
                if (c != null) {
                    r.contactId = c.Id;
                    r.found = true;
                } else {
                    r.contactId = null;
                    r.found = false;
                }
            } else {
                r.contactId = null;
                r.found = false;
            }
            results.add(r);
        }

        return results;
    }

    public class Request {
        @InvocableVariable(label='Email' required=true)
        public String email;
    }

    public class Result {
        @InvocableVariable(label='Contact Id')
        public Id contactId;

        @InvocableVariable(label='Found')
        public Boolean found;
    }
}
```

**Key design decisions:**

| Concern | How it's handled |
|---|---|
| **Case-insensitive match** | SOQL `IN` is case-sensitive, so after the query returns candidates we build a map keyed on `Email.toLowerCase()` and look up with the lowercased input. This correctly handles any casing difference between the stored value and the input. |
| **Trim / blank** | Input is trimmed before the set is built; blank/null emails are skipped entirely and immediately get `found = false`. |
| **Earliest CreatedDate** | The query uses `ORDER BY CreatedDate ASC`; the Apex loop keeps only the *first* contact seen per lowercased email, which is guaranteed to be the earliest. |
| **One SOQL query** | A single `SELECT … WHERE Email IN :emailSet` executes once regardless of batch size (up to 200). |
| **Order preservation** | Results are built by iterating the original request list by index. |
| **Sharing** | The class is declared `with sharing` (the Apex default, stated explicitly for clarity), so the query respects the running user's sharing rules. |
| **Bulk safety** | No per-record queries; the entire batch is resolved with one query and in-memory maps. |

