public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {

    /**
     * For any new line whose Discount is null, apply the standard discount
     * from PricingRules based on the product's family.
     *
     * Bulk-safe: issues at most one SOQL query (Product2 lookup) regardless
     * of how many lines are being inserted.
     */
    public static void handleBeforeInsert(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        // 1. Identify lines that need a default discount
        List<OpportunityLineItem> linesNeedingDiscount = new List<OpportunityLineItem>();
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                linesNeedingDiscount.add(line);
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }

        if (linesNeedingDiscount.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. Bulk-query Product2 for the families we need (single SOQL)
        Map<Id, String> idToFamily = new Map<Id, String>();
        for (Product2 p : [SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :productIds]) {
            idToFamily.put(p.Id, p.Family);
        }

        // 3. Apply the standard discount via PricingRules (no SOQL in this method)
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : linesNeedingDiscount) {
            String family = idToFamily.get(line.Product2Id);
            line.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(family);
        }
    }
}
```

## My take

I'd do it slightly differently: instead of the nested loop, I'd build a `Map<String, Id>` of normalised email → first contact Id, then do a single lookup per lead. That turns the worst case from **O(n × m)** (up to 9 M iterations) into **O(n + m)** (up to 6 K operations). In Apex, 9 M string comparisons with a helper method call each is doable but starts to eat into the 10 M ms CPU limit, especially if the import calls this more than once per transaction or the lists grow. The Map version is equally readable—it's the standard Apex pattern for this kind of in-memory join—and it's future-proof if the "few thousand" becomes "few tens of thousands."

The only trade-off is a one-time pass over the contacts to build the map, which is negligible.

