public class RenewalReminderService {

    /**
     * Sends a renewal-reminder email to the owner of each specified opportunity.
     * One Messaging.sendEmail call per opportunity so that a failure on one
     * does not prevent the remaining emails from being sent.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds  Up to 200 Opportunity Ids.
     * @return The number of emails that were sent successfully.
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppMap = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId, CloseDate
             FROM Opportunity
             WHERE Id IN :opportunityIds]
        );

        Integer sentCount = 0;

        for (Opportunity opp : oppMap.values()) {
            if (opp.OwnerId == null) {
                continue;
            }

            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage msg = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            msg.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            msg.setSaveAsActivity(false);
            msg.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
            msg.setPlainTextBody(
                'Hi,\n\n'
                + 'The opportunity "' + opp.Name + '" has a close date of '
                + String.valueOf(opp.CloseDate)
                + '. Please follow up with the customer regarding the renewal.\n\n'
                + 'Regards'
            );

            try {
                EmailResult[] results = Messaging.sendEmail(
                    new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>{ msg }
                );
                if (results != null && results.size() > 0 && results[0].isSuccess()) {
                    sentCount++;
                } else {
                    String statusMsg = (results != null && results.size() > 0)
                        ? results[0].getStatus() : 'no result returned';
                    System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                        'RenewalReminder: sendEmail returned non-success for '
                        + opp.Id + ' – ' + statusMsg);
                }
            } catch (Exception e) {
                System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR,
                    'RenewalReminder: exception sending reminder for '
                    + opp.Id + ' – ' + e.getMessage());
            }
        }

        return sentCount;
    }
}
```

### Key design decisions

| Concern | Decision |
|---|---|
| Bulk-safety | Single SOQL query for all IDs; no per-row DML. |
| Isolation | One `sendEmail` call per opportunity inside a `try/catch`, so a failure on one record never aborts the rest. |
| Success counting | Check both that the call didn't throw **and** that `EmailResult.isSuccess()` is true (covers the case where the call returns a failure status rather than throwing). |
| Null owner | Skipped silently; no point sending to a null target. |
| `setSaveAsActivity(false)` | Keeps the activity feed clean; the scheduled job's purpose is notification, not audit. |

## Explanation of the change to your approach

Your instinct to do **one query, no queries in loops** is exactly right, and I've kept that. However, I've changed *what* that one query selects: instead of loading **all 2.3 million contacts**, it loads only the contacts belonging to the accounts whose `BillingCountry` actually changed in the current transaction.

Why:

| Concern | All 2.3 M contacts | Scoped query |
|---|---|---|
| SOQL row limit (sync) | 10 000 → **immediately violated** | Typically a few hundred to a few thousand |
| Heap (6 MB sync / 12 MB async) | 2.3 M sObjects → **guaranteed OOM** | Well within limits |
| Performance | Scans the entire Contact table | Index seek on `AccountId` |
| Correctness | Same logic, just wasted work | Same logic |

The query `[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact WHERE AccountId IN :changedIds]` is still **one SOQL, issued once, outside any loop** — it satisfies your bulk-safety requirement while staying within governor limits for a batch of 200 accounts.

